WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.220
Why would one ion channel need 114 transmembrane helices?

2
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.440
The first map came in 2015, from Ge and colleagues.

3
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.700
It showed a trimer with three propeller-like arms.

4
00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:21.370
Zhao and colleagues resolved each arm into repeating units of four helices.

5
00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.890
Those units form curved blades around a central pore.

6
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:30.130
They also mutated single residues of a long beam inside the cell.

7
00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:32.870
Mechanical activation was impaired.

8
00:00:33.883 --> 00:00:38.103
So that part of the structure matters for gating, not just for building the channel.

9
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:44.553
By 2019, a structure of mouse Piezo2 was sharp enough to count the helices.

10
00:00:45.583 --> 00:00:48.703
Each subunit holds 38 transmembrane helices.

11
00:00:49.383 --> 00:00:51.863
The three-subunit channel holds 114.

12
00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:55.087
Keep the two numbers apart.

13
00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:57.667
Thirty-eight describes one subunit.

14
00:00:58.547 --> 00:01:01.267
One hundred and fourteen describes one channel.

15
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.610
Those helices do something unusual.

16
00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:07.690
The curved blades bow the membrane into a dome.

17
00:01:08.683 --> 00:01:14.083
In purified mouse Piezo2, the dome is reported as about twenty-eight nanometres

18
00:01:14.083 --> 00:01:15.623
across and ten deep.

19
00:01:17.683 --> 00:01:19.143
A dome has two areas.

20
00:01:20.023 --> 00:01:22.563
Its surface area is the membrane actually in it.

21
00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:27.113
Its projected area is its shadow on the flat plane around it.

22
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:30.193
That is the area it occupies, seen from above.

23
00:01:31.233 --> 00:01:32.953
Treat the dome as a smooth cap.

24
00:01:33.973 --> 00:01:35.213
Flatten it without stretching it.

25
00:01:35.913 --> 00:01:37.293
Does its shadow grow or shrink?

26
00:01:38.193 --> 00:01:39.193
By roughly how much?

27
00:01:45.183 --> 00:01:50.843
It grows. The curved cap casts a shadow of about six hundred and sixteen square

28
00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:51.843
nanometres.

29
00:01:52.433 --> 00:01:56.673
Flattened, its whole surface lies in the plane, about nine hundred and thirty.

30
00:01:57.513 --> 00:02:00.193
The gain is about three hundred and ten square nanometres.

31
00:02:04.317 --> 00:02:07.577
The gain depends on how deep the dome is, not on how wide.

32
00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:11.347
This is idealized geometry, not a measurement.

33
00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:15.827
The real dome is protein blades and bent lipid, not a smooth cap.

34
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.180
So flattening increases projected area.

35
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.680
Anything that favours more projected area favours the flat state.

36
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.020
In the leading model, still debated, flattening is thought to help open the pore.

37
00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:33.373
In the leading model, a dome is a device for gaining area.
